lesson physics-falling-and-floating v=1 format=teach grade=4 unit=1 subject=physics levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Falling and floating"
  goal "Use the forces on a falling toy and a floating boat to say what they will do next, and tell a fair test of a parachute from an unfair one."

  look {
    scene 31x15 {
      row both at=canvas(1, 0) space=2 align=start {
        parachute p canopy=5 weight=6 drag=6
        boat b cargo=3 side=6 sits=1 arrows=1
      }
    }
    say """
      Gravity pulls everything down: that pull is its weight. Falling through air, a thing is pushed
      back up by the air, and a wide canopy catches more of it. When the push up is less than the
      weight it speeds up; when they are equal it falls steadily. A floating boat is held up by the
      water's push, which is exactly as big as its weight, however much it is carrying.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice physics.parachute-fall count=2 seed=3
    practice physics.parachute-slower count=2 seed=1
    practice physics.boat-weight count=2 seed=2
    practice physics.boat-more count=2 seed=4
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show physics.parachute-fair x=3 y=7 m=4 n=6 easy-n=4
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show physics.parachute-steady w=8 c=5 easy-w=4 hard-w=10
  }
  remember {
    say "Weight pulls down. Air and water push up. When the two are equal, nothing speeds up or slows down."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Make two parachutes from a supermarket bag, one twice the size of the other, with the same toy on
    each, and drop them from the top of the stairs. Then tape a coin to one toy and ask whether that
    drop still says anything about the canopy. The steady-speed question at the end is the idea the
    whole lesson rests on, balanced forces, and many children expect the answer to be nothing.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the weight and the air's push are far apart, the canopies compared differ by three squares or more, and the two drops are a fair test."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the weight and the push are within two newtons or equal, the canopies differ by one square, and the steady fall carries ten newtons."
  }
}
